  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Thunderstorms winding down this afternoon.  \nAdditional storms are expected into the evening, but will be \nmuch less trouble. \n\nJust a couple of showers across Clinton and Lycoming Counties at\n5 PM, and while they have lost their lightning, they are\nspinning and one has split into two. So, we'll continue to keep\nan eye on them. Most of the showers and a few storms will stay\nover the western third of the state for the rest of the night.\nJust an isolated SHRA/TSRA is expected elsewhere. \n\nPrevious...\n\nMCS moving off to our E at 4 PM. Only a few of the SE Counties\nare left in the watch, as they still have some potential left\nfor severe wx. Some instability remains across the rest of the \nCWA. But, that is of no concern for severe wx. Can't rule out a\nheavy downpour out of one of these stragglers or something that\nmay develop this evening. But, the overall threats for SVR and\nFF are very low. \n\n-----------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Humid with rounds of heavy showers and strong \nthunderstorms focused over southwest Pennsylvania through \nmidweek\n\nModel trends are focusing primary risk for severe storms and\nheavy rain across southwest PA on Tuesday. This area lies\nclosest to the equatorward edge of the westerlies within very\nmoist instability gradient. SPC and WPC have outlooked this \narea (SW PA) with a a level 2/5 severe storm risk and 2/4 \nexcessive rain risk. \n\nRounds of showers and storms remain possible through midweek. \nMultiple shortwave disturbances will traverse the northern tier\nand interact with a slow moving frontal zone. The combination \nof rich low level moisture, instability and repeated forcing \nalong the boundary will support some potential for periodic \nclusters of storms with locally heavy rainfall. WPC excessive \nrain outlooks (EROs) have shifted farther to the south/west and \nonly clip the far southwest portion of the CWA on days 3-5 or \nWed-Fri. \n\nThe end of the week into the start of the weekend looks less\nhumid (more refreshing) as push of relatively cooler and drier\nair accompanies seasonably strong/Canadian high pressure \nbuilding southeast from the Upper Midwest.",
